<header><p class="st-kicker">other · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Cisplatina</h1><p class="st-meta">1817 CE – 1828 CE · capital Montevideo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The province of Uruguay under Portuguese and then Brazilian rule, whose struggle for freedom led to the independence of Uruguay.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Portuguese forces from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a> invaded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-province">Banda Oriental</a> in 1816 to crush <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-gervasio-artigas">José Artigas</a>, and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-frederico-lecor">Carlos Frederico Lecor</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> in January 1817, ending the Eastern Province. In 1821 a congress in Montevideo, under Portuguese pressure, voted to annex the territory as the Cisplatine Province.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-frederico-lecor">Lecor</a> governed with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo's</a> merchants and landowners. When <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-republic-brazil">Brazil</a> declared independence in 1822, Cisplatina passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Empire of Brazil</a> after Portuguese troops in Montevideo were expelled in 1824. In 1825 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-three-orientals">Thirty-Three Orientals</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-antonio-lavalleja">Juan Antonio Lavalleja</a> landed and declared union with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata</a>, sparking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisplatine-war">Cisplatine War</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Neither side could win: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina's</a> victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ituzaingo">Ituzaingó</a> in 1827 was offset by a Brazilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>. Through British mediation, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preliminary-peace-convention">Preliminary Peace Convention</a> of 1828 created the independent <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Oriental Republic of Uruguay</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cisplatina.jpg" alt="Cisplatina" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Variant of the flag designed by José Gervasio Artigas and José María de Roo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Merchant_ensign_of_Cisplatine_Province.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1817 CE – 1828 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Montevideo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Carlos Frederico Lecor</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/uruguay">Uruguay</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cisplatine Province, Província Cisplatina</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1822&amp;focus=cisplatina" aria-label="Cisplatina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3255 3440 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cisplatina lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3575 3696L3845 3279M3575 3696L3846 3279M3575 3696L3575 3696M3575 3696L3575 3696"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3845 3279h0M3846 3279h0M3575 3696h0M3575 3696h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3575 3696h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3575 3696h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Montevideo. <a href="/atlas?year=1822&amp;focus=cisplatina">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
